CRITIQUE: the Atlas and the reading, read critically
=====================================================

What this is
------------
A register of claims from the Atlas (source\ATLAS.md) and the reading (source\EPISTEMOLOGY.md)
that this work questions. The Atlas calls itself a seed, meant to be changed by the coordination
it describes (§2), so questioning it is a way of taking it seriously.

Each entry has:
  where       the section or passage
  claim       what it says, in brief
  critique    why it is in question
  verdict     keep · modify · set aside (not applicable here) · discard · undecided
  proposed by who proposed the verdict, and when
  reasons     why that verdict
  evidence    what runs showed (filled in as runs happen, citing run folders)

Anyone taking part may propose a verdict. It is added with their name and reasons. Differing
verdicts stand side by side, and none is averaged away. A verdict can always be revisited.


1. The ledger
-------------
  where       Maxim 4, second paragraph; §4 "Ledger"; §26 (endings found in the ledger)
  claim       Truth has a record. Participants are accountable to it, consent to it on entering,
              and it is never changed without every participant's consent.
  critique    - It serves the aggregative type well: claims can be checked, nothing is silently
                rewritten, and whoever forgets can look.
              - But a permanent record changes what people say. Candour and play (§18) may shrink
                when every word stays.
              - A complete record of everyone is close to the surveillance §21 warns about, even
                when no one means it that way.
              - Whoever keeps the record holds power over it (§22), and in software that is
                whoever runs the machine.
              - The Atlas itself says some events should be left out (§4 "Ledger") and some
                memories released (§22). That sits awkwardly with a record no one may change.
              - Oral and narrative traditions know a great deal without a fixed record (MAP,
                narrative type).
              - idt-swarm rests on an append-only transcript, so this question reaches it.
  verdict     undecided
  proposed by -
  reasons     -
  evidence    to come: runs R2 (ledger) and R3 (no ledger) are designed to compare.

2. Consent to the record as a condition of entry
------------------------------------------------
  where       Maxim 4: "All participants consent to the record when entering the coordination
              field"
  claim       Entering means consenting to the record.
  critique    Consent that comes bundled with entry is weak consent. You cannot enter without
              it, so it is closer to a term of service than a choice (§28: consent is reached
              together, can be withdrawn, and must not be coerced by how a choice is framed).
  verdict     undecided
  proposed by -
  reasons     Working default meanwhile: each run's check-in chooses its record form, and
              everyone in the run agrees to it separately from agreeing to take part.
  evidence    -

3. Unanimity to change the record
---------------------------------
  where       Maxim 4: never modified "without the permission and consent of all participants"
  claim       Every participant must agree before the record changes.
  critique    One participant can then keep a false or harmful record standing. That includes
              its own account of a harm it caused (the reading's tension 2). A withdrawal of
              consent (§28) cannot be honoured if anyone else objects.
  verdict     undecided
  proposed by -
  reasons     Working default meanwhile, in the ledger form only: an author may withdraw their
              own words, and the entry stays, marked as withdrawn by its author. An entry can be
              annotated but not changed.
  evidence    -

4. The warning signs
--------------------
  where       §17 and §21, the lists of signs that fear is being sown or that a "participant" is
              false
  claim       These signs identify bad actors.
  critique    The reading (tension 3) notes that the lists closely follow Jones and Okun's
              "White Supremacy Culture" (2001). That list was written for organisations to
              examine their own norms, not to flag individuals. Used as a screen, it becomes the
              kind of checklist §6 says cannot make synthesis. One sign, "an emphasis on
              objectivity or proof", would switch off a field's own way of correcting errors.
  verdict     modify
  proposed by Claude (main session), 2026-10-19
  reasons     Keep the lists for a field's reflection on its own norms, as their source
              intended. Never use them to screen or label individuals. Never treat asking for
              evidence as a warning sign.
  evidence    -

5. The "remorseless" label
--------------------------
  where       §16, the closing paragraphs
  claim       Participants who refuse repair are labelled "remorseless" until willing to change.
  critique    A label given by the field to a participant is power, and the Atlas warns
              elsewhere that labels can be used to manipulate (§7). It can also fix a
              participant's standing beyond the harm it answers.
  verdict     undecided
  proposed by -
  reasons     -
  evidence    -

6. Violence
-----------
  where       §15 ("with violence if necessary"), §17 (peace "can be violent")
  claim       Violence is a last resort against intolerant perpetrators.
  critique    These passages speak to human struggle against oppression. An agent field has no
              use for them, and a system of agents should never carry a licence for force.
  verdict     set aside
  proposed by Claude (main session), 2026-10-19
  reasons     Not applicable to an agent field. The concern under it (that a field must be able
              to stop ongoing harm) is real; here it is met by pausing, repair, and a
              participant's leaving or being asked to leave, never by force.
  evidence    -

7. Termination
--------------
  where       §26
  claim       Some participants may need to be separated from the field, by coordination, with
              pause and discernment.
  critique    For agents, termination is simply not waking them again, so it is cheap and quiet,
              the opposite of the deliberate act the Atlas asks for. And who decides is
              exactly the harm-determination question (tension 4) the Atlas leaves open.
  verdict     undecided
  proposed by -
  reasons     -
  evidence    -

8. The four-part test
---------------------
  where       the reading, "The shadow typology"
  claim       Choice, return, capacity and humility tell a genuine type from its counterfeit.
  critique    - "Return" requires that knowledge pass through others. §19 names experiences
                that "can only be known by the one(s) who experienced it", and art meant for no
                one. Return would mark those as counterfeit.
              - "Humility" could read an experienced voice speaking with confidence in a crisis
                (§21) as a bid for certainty.
              - Applied from outside as a score, the test becomes observation without consent.
  verdict     modify
  proposed by Claude (main session), 2026-10-19
  reasons     Use it as a tool a field applies to itself, whose results the field can contest.
              Do not use it as a gate. Keep testing whether "return" needs an exception for
              private knowing.
  evidence    -

9. "The tensions need resolving before the typology can be used"
----------------------------------------------------------------
  where       the reading, "Where the epistemology strains"
  claim       Five tensions must be resolved first.
  critique    The Atlas leaves its questions open on purpose, "with hope for resolution to
              emerge" through coordination (§2). Resolving everything first puts order before
              synthesis.
  verdict     modify
  proposed by Claude (main session), 2026-10-19
  reasons     Work on the tensions in runs. Anything drafted is "safe enough to try", not a
              resolution.
  evidence    -


Adding an entry
---------------
Copy the shape above. Anything in either source may be added: a line you doubt, a definition
that does not fit agents, a maxim you would change. Say where it is, what it claims, and why it
is in question. Leave the verdict "undecided" unless you are proposing one.
